Master Prompt: CDS Control Tower — Full-Stack Development

Standalone React UI + Existing Spring Boot Backend + Premium Multi-Theme Design

Act as a Senior Full-Stack Engineer, React Architect, Spring Boot Developer, Oracle SQL Engineer, and Enterprise UI/UX Designer.

I am developing a CDS Control Tower application. I want a modern, professional, colorful, interactive dashboard while reusing my existing Spring Boot backend and its Oracle database configuration.

The existing application uses an older internal "ssc-cdt4" framework, which may not support modern frontend technologies or styling properly. Therefore, I want the dashboard frontend to be a separate, standalone React application with its own "package.json", dependencies, source code, and build process, integrated into the existing Spring Boot project through Maven/npm scripting.

The solution must be modular, maintainable, compatible with the existing backend, and easy to plug in without unnecessarily changing existing backend functionality.

---

1. First: Inspect the Existing Project

Before making any changes:

- Inspect the complete project structure, "pom.xml", existing frontend configuration, Java/Spring Boot versions, Node/npm versions, and current build scripts.
- Identify how the existing "ssc-cdt4" framework is used and which parts of it must remain untouched.
- Inspect existing Oracle DB configuration, entity/model classes, repositories, services, controllers, and API conventions.
- Identify existing Maven plugins and determine how a standalone React build can be integrated.
- Do not assume that the latest versions of React, Vite, Tailwind CSS, or other libraries are compatible with the existing environment.
- Check available versions and dependencies in the organization’s npm registry before selecting the stack.
- Reuse existing backend DB configuration and services wherever possible.

Do not overwrite or migrate the existing application architecture without a clear reason.

First provide an implementation plan and explain the proposed folder structure, integration approach, and compatibility considerations. Then implement incrementally.

