Before choosing dependencies, verify that each required package and compatible version is available through the organization’s configured npm registry.

Inspect:

- Current registry configuration using "npm config get registry".
- ".npmrc" configuration and any organization-specific registry settings.
- Node.js and npm versions.
- Package availability, versions, peer dependencies, and compatibility.
- Whether the registry has cached or approved versions of required packages.

Check availability for the proposed stack, including:

- "react"
- "react-dom"
- "vite"
- "@vitejs/plugin-react"
- "tailwindcss"
- Tailwind’s required Vite integration package for the selected version
- "lucide-react" for icons
- A charting library such as "recharts"
- "axios" or the project’s existing HTTP client, if appropriate

Use registry commands such as:

npm config get registry
node --version
npm --version
npm view react versions
npm view vite versions
npm view tailwindcss versions
npm view lucide-react versions
npm view recharts versions
npm view axios versions

Also verify the required package metadata and peer dependencies for the exact versions selected.

Do not assume that a package is available merely because it exists on the public npm registry. Use the registry configured in the organization.

If a package is unavailable, identify an approved alternative or ask before introducing a new dependency.

Deliver a dependency compatibility report showing the selected package versions, registry availability, compatibility notes, and any alternatives considered.

